JF Mezei wrote:
> Bradford Hamilton wrote:
> 
>> For example, my wife will be purchasing a MacBook laptop, to replace her iBook.
>> 4X original memory and disk capacity, plus a newer OS, to boot.
> 
> A 400mhz CPU can't really handle MPEG-4 decompression in real-time. It
> can handle Mpeg-2 (DVDs), but the newer compression techniques are more
> CPU intensive and require more power in order to playback movies in real
> time without freezups.
> 
> And those compression techniques result in variable data rates. If, for
> 20 seconds, you see one person talking to another with only the mouth
> changing, this will require far less data be read from disk than some
> sports scene with camera panning while lots of players with complex
> colour schemes on their jerseys playing around.
> 
> So, for those applications, you do need the extra CPU (and probably memory).
> 
> Consider the fancy 3D games, those also require a hefty amount of
> resources on a PC.
> 
> But for word processing, current machines are more than sufficiently
> powerful. (unless MS starts to force people to stop using keyboards and
> dictate the text they want to put in, at which point, the extra CPU will
> be required).

Speech recognition technology will have to get a LOT better before that 
is practical.

I have a cellular phone that is supposed to take voice commands.  Maybe 
it works for some people but it certainly does not work for me.  I can 
tell the phone "Check Status" and get the expected result about one time 
in fifty!  The rest of the time it tries to dial a number and I have to 
do some fast button pushing to avoid being charged for a toll call!

Some VERY "high end" speech recognition works fairly well but most of it
is not very dependable!


